WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:07.169
Now that we've got an understanding of what the User Widget is, let's take a look at Unreal's inbuilt tool for visually authoring them.

2
00:00:07.169 --> 00:00:10.169
But firstly, how do we create a new widget?

3
00:00:10.169 --> 00:00:16.300
Simply right-click in the content browser of your project and click Widget Blueprint under the User Interface category.

4
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:22.460
This will create a new widget, extending off of User Widget by default, as an asset in your content browser.

5
00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:29.620
Opening this asset will summon the Widget Blueprint editor, and this is our authoring tool for developing UI with UMG.

6
00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:33.619
We can see straight away that we've got a whole suite of menus and toolboxes to explore.

7
00:00:33.619 --> 00:00:37.869
Right at the top in section 1, we've got the Common Toolbar.

8
00:00:37.869 --> 00:00:41.869
This is pretty much the standard toolbar that we'd find in any of the existing tools within Unreal,

9
00:00:41.869 --> 00:00:45.939
and we have access to File, Editor, Help, it's the usual suspects.

10
00:00:45.939 --> 00:00:48.939
Underneath that in section 2, we've got the Toolbar.

11
00:00:48.939 --> 00:00:54.969
This contains a number of common functions that we also find in the regular Blueprint editor, that being Compile, Save and Play.

12
00:00:54.969 --> 00:00:58.509
Section 3 is the Editor Mode Selection.

13
00:00:58.509 --> 00:01:03.509
This lets us swap between the Designer View, which is what we're currently looking at, and a more typical Blueprint graph view.

14
00:01:03.509 --> 00:01:09.769
Each widget has got a Designer Front End, a Blueprint Back End, and a C++ Base under the hood,

15
00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:13.769
much in the same way that an Actor Blueprint has got a viewport view instead of a Designer.

16
00:01:13.769 --> 00:01:16.769
The same sort of architecture is at play here.

17
00:01:16.769 --> 00:01:20.439
On the left in section 4, we've got the Palette.

18
00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:23.439
This is a list of widgets that can be dragged and dropped into the Visual Designer,

19
00:01:23.439 --> 00:01:26.439
and will display anything that inherits from UWidget.

20
00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:33.569
This will also include any containers such as vertical and horizontal boxes, scroll boxes, overlays, borders, spaces.

21
00:01:33.569 --> 00:01:36.569
It's the usual toolset for any visual UI design.

22
00:01:36.569 --> 00:01:39.980
It'll also contain any user generated widgets.

23
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:43.980
So say we've got a health bar already made up, and now we're working on a Master HUD widget.

24
00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:49.980
We can drag a health bar into the Master HUD widget by simply navigating to the user created category,

25
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.980
and selecting the widget that we'd like.

26
00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:56.560
Below the Palette, we've got the Hierarchy view of section 5.

27
00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.560
This view is a tree view of the parenting structure of the current widget,

28
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.560
and for ease, we can also drag directly from the Palette into the Hierarchy view,

29
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.819
to position new widgets exactly where we'd like them.

30
00:02:07.819 --> 00:02:11.979
From there, we've got section 6, which is the Visual Designer proper.

31
00:02:11.979 --> 00:02:16.979
This is a visual representation of the layout, with each visual element viable for manipulation once placed.

32
00:02:16.979 --> 00:02:22.979
That can be moving it around, changing properties, deleting, resizing, or even reparenting to other widgets.

33
00:02:22.979 --> 00:02:28.259
Section 7 is the Details panel, and here we can see information about the currently selected widget,

34
00:02:28.259 --> 00:02:31.259
and change any of its exposed properties.

35
00:02:31.259 --> 00:02:34.259
For instance, we could freely change the U texture source of an image widget.

36
00:02:34.259 --> 00:02:39.969
Finally, in section 8, it's where we can author keyframed animations for widgets.

37
00:02:39.969 --> 00:02:42.969
So say we want some UI to bounce onto the screen when we click a button.

38
00:02:42.969 --> 00:02:45.969
This is where we'd actually author that bounce.

39
00:02:45.969 --> 00:02:50.639
We'll touch on animation actually quite heavily, in more detail later on in this talk.

40
00:02:50.639 --> 00:02:55.639
Just as a quick example, we can see here that we've got an image widget being dragged into the Visual Designer.

41
00:02:55.639 --> 00:02:59.639
We're navigating over to the Details panel, and selecting a U texture source.

42
00:02:59.639 --> 00:03:04.639
We can also see in the Hierarchy view, that it's updated to show that we've now got an image widget

43
00:03:04.639 --> 00:03:09.639
as a child of the Canvas panel, which is the base container for all widgets by default.

44
00:03:09.639 --> 00:03:12.699
Authoring widgets in UMG is exceptionally easy.

45
00:03:12.699 --> 00:03:14.699
If we were to spawn this class and add it to the viewport,

46
00:03:14.699 --> 00:03:18.740
we'd see that exact same star with the exact same offset from the top left.

47
00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.740
As you can see, if we switch over to the Graph view instead of the Designer view,

48
00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:25.740
we're dealing with the same framework that we've got right across the engine.

49
00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.740
That being a blueprint backend to some kind of visual frontend.

50
00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:33.740
This holds true for the animation graphs, the actor graphs, and now the UMG graphs.

51
00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:38.740
We've got access to a series of contextual nodes to drive any logic that we may want our UI to run.
